All Work And No Play Makes Jack a Dull Boy: Understanding and Preventing Catastrophic Strategy Collapse in RLVR Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! Learn more × Back to arXiv License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.02835v1 [cs.LG] 02 Oct 2026 2 2 footnotetext: These authors contributed equally to this work. ∗ Corresponding author: meng.li@pku.edu.cn All Work And No Play Makes Jack a Dull Boy: Understanding and Preventing Catastrophic Strategy Collapse in RLVR Qiyuan HuangPeking University Tianshi XuPeking University Meng Li * Peking University Abstract During post-training of large language models (LLMs) with Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR), GRPO-style algorithms can exhibit severe late-stage collapse. Prompt-based probing reveals that this is not benign strategic pruning, but a harmful contraction of effective strategy capacity that makes distinct reasoning strategies increasingly inaccessible. To characterize this phenomenon, we define strategies through trajectory-level policy-update interactions and develop a unified theoretical framework combining optimization dynamics and information theory. We prove that major RLVR objectives progressively concentrate probability mass onto a single strategy, while sustaining nontrivial task accuracy requires a minimum strategy capacity. The conflict between these two results provides a mechanistic explanation for catastrophic collapse. We further derive the Mirrored Entanglement Index (MEI) as a lightweight online warning signal. To prevent collapse, we propose Mesh Learning , which exposes multiple reasoning strategies and prevents any single strategy from dominating optimization. Across AIME26, AIME25, MATH-500, GPQA, and LiveCodeBench, Mesh Learning consistently outperforms strong baselines across Qwen and Phi model families, with gains of up to 13.4 pp and 11.5 pp, respectively. These results establish strategy preservation as a key principle for stable RLVR. Code is available at GitHub . Figure 1: Left: Baseline RLVR progressively collapses to a single strategy, while Mesh Learning preserves strategy diversity through rebalancing. Right: On AIME26, all baselines eventually collapse, whereas Mesh Learning remains stable and accurate across two model families. 1 Introduction In recent years, Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) has emerged as a powerful paradigm for improving the complex reasoning capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs), particularly in mathematics and coding ( Guo et al., 2025 ; Chen et al., 2025 ; Xu et al., 2026a ; Team et al., 2026 ; Zeng et al., 2026 ; Xu et al., 2026b ) . Driven by verifiable outcome supervision and scalable critic-free policy optimization, RLVR has been rapidly adopted in both academia and industry, giving rise to a range of optimization frameworks, including GRPO ( Guo et al., 2025 ) , DAPO ( Yu et al., 2025 ) , and GSPO ( Zheng et al., 2025 ) , as well as regularization schemes based on KL divergence ( Shao et al., 2024 ) and JS divergence ( Li et al., 2026 ) . Together, these developments have established RLVR as a core post-training paradigm for modern reasoning models. Despite its effectiveness in improving reasoning performance, RLVR can exhibit severe late-stage instability. Prior studies have reported similar collapse behaviors ( Guo et al., 2025 ; Zheng et al., 2025 ; Gao et al., 2025 ; Li et al., 2026 ) , and we observe the same phenomenon systematically in our experiments. As shown in Fig. 1 , accuracy drops abruptly after a period of seemingly successful optimization across two model families, Qwen ( Yang et al., 2025 ) and Phi ( Abdin et al., 2025 ) , and five representative training recipes, including GRPO, DAPO, GSPO, GRPO+KL, and GRPO+JS. We call this abrupt degradation collapse . Its recurrence across prior studies, model families, algorithms, and regularization schemes suggests a shared limitation of current RLVR optimization, yet its underlying mechanism remains poorly understood. To characterize this behavior across RLVR algorithms, we develop a unified theory grounded in the optimization dynamics of policy updates. Since individual trajectories rarely recur exactly and are too fine-grained to describe reasoning behavior, we introduce strategies as groups of trajectories that evolve coherently under parameter updates, and derive their distribution updates from the underlying trajectory-level updates. Within this framework, we explain collapse by contradiction through two central theorems: 1) If RLVR objectives (GRPO, DAPO, GSPO) persist in a non-collapse regime, the optimization inherently drives the policy toward a single dominant strategy with high probability; 2) achieving nontrivial task accuracy requires the model to retain a minimum strategy capacity. Together, these results reveal a fundamental conflict: RLVR optimization progressively contracts strategy capacity, while successful reasoning requires sufficient capacity to remain available. This contradiction provides a mechanistic explanation for the catastrophic collapse observed in practice. This mechanistic view also suggests what to monitor before accuracy collapses: the latent concentration of strategy dynamics. Since this transition is difficult to observe from standard training signals, we derive the Mirrored Entanglement Index (MEI) from the geometry of RLVR updates as a lightweight online proxy for strategy collapse, requiring no auxiliary training and little computational overhead. Calibrating MEI on pre-RLVR models yields a 3 ​ σ 3\sigma alarm threshold for detecting departure from the non-collapsed regime before the accuracy cliff. Across model families, RLVR algorithms, and regularization schemes, MEI rises above the threshold ahead of the eventual accuracy cliff, consistently tracking the theoretically predicted transition. Independent strategy-level analysis further confirms that this transition corresponds to the loss of distinct reasoning strategies, supporting strategy collapse as a shared mechanism underlying late-stage RLVR failure. Building on the theoretical characterization and online measurability of strategy collapse, we propose Mesh Learning , a strategy-preserving RLVR framework that prevents individual strategies from dominating optimization and preserves multiple viable reasoning strategies throughout training. It consists of two complementary components: 1) Coach Prompting (CP) , which augments each query with strategy prefixes that elicit distinct solution approaches and ensure broad strategy exposure; and 2) Strategy-Balancing Regularization , which balances the learning progress across strategies to prevent any single strategy from overwhelming the others. In this way, CP constructs a diverse strategy mesh, while the regularizer preserves it throughout optimization. Across AIME26, AIME25, MATH-500 ( Hendrycks et al., 2021 ; Lightman et al., 2023 ) , GPQA ( Rein et al., 2024 ) , and LiveCodeBench (v6) ( Jain et al., 2024 ) , Mesh Learning consistently outperforms strong RLVR and divergence-regularized baselines across both Qwen and Phi families, with gains of up to 13.4 and 11.5 percentage points (pp), respectively. These results highlight strategy preservation as a key principle for stable and effective RLVR optimization. In summary, our contributions are as follows: ❶ We establish a unified theory of RLVR dynamics, showing that inherent competition drives strategy concentration and explaining collapse through its conflict with the minimum strategy capacity required for successful reasoning. The analysis also yields MEI as a lightweight online indicator. ❷ We propose Mesh Learning , combining Coach Prompting and Strategy-Balancing Regularization to expose diverse reasoning strategies and prevent any strategy from dominating optimization. ❸ We provide extensive empirical validation across RLVR recipes and model families on AIME25/26, MATH-500, GPQA-Diamond, and LiveCodeBench, confirming the predicted collapse dynamics and the effectiveness of Mesh Learning. 2 From Trajectory to Strategy Since the exact recurrence of an individual trajectory is exceedingly unlikely, analyzing isolated trajectories provides limited insight into training dynamics. To study these dynamics more effectively, we introduce the notion of a strategy , which captures a group of trajectories that respond coherently to local policy updates, i.e., they tend to be reinforced or suppressed together. 2.1 The Concept of Strategy Consider a prompt Q Q and a policy π θ \pi_{	heta} parameterized by θ 	heta . For a query Q Q , let its admissible trajectory space be 𝒯 Q = { τ 1 , τ 2 , … } \mathcal{T}_{Q}=\{	au_{1},	au_{2},\ldots\} . We first introduce the Fisher Score of a trajectory τ i 	au_{i} : U i = ∇ θ ​ log ​ π θ ​ ( τ i ∣ Q ) . U_{i}=
