lly distinct strategy prefixes, each specifying a candidate method, optionally with a brief description, but excluding calculations, intermediate solutions, and final answers. Each prefix conditions a separate rollout group, ensuring explicit exploration of all m m strategies rather than relying on spontaneous policy discovery. These prefixes are generated once and fixed throughout training, incurring no online Coach cost. During training, each prefix is treated as a candidate rather than a fixed instruction: the policy assesses its applicability, underlying mechanism, and alternatives before answering. At inference, no pre-generated prefix is provided; the policy proposes a candidate strategy itself and follows the same verification procedure, requiring no Coach LLM at test time. Coach-provided tokens are masked from the training loss. Full prompts and implementation details are provided in Appendix D.1 . 4.2 Strategy-Balancing Regularization We seek to prevent strategy elimination without hindering their joint improvement. For strategy S i S_{i} , let 𝒞 i ⊂ S i \mathcal{C}_{i}\subset S_{i} denote its correct trajectories, and define z i = 1 | 𝒞 i | ​ ∑ τ ∈ 𝒞 i log ⁡ π θ ​ ( τ ∣ Q ) z_{i}= rac{1}{|\mathcal{C}_{i}|}\sum_{	au\in\mathcal{C}_{i}}\log\pi_{	heta}(	au\mid Q) as a differentiable surrogate for its current support under the policy. Let 𝐳 = ( z 1 , … , z m ) \mathbf{z}=(z_{1},\ldots,z_{m}) and let 𝒰 m \mathcal{U}_{m} denote the uniform distribution over the m m strategies. Directly minimizing D KL ( 𝒰 m ∥ softmax ( 𝐳 ) ) D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\mathcal{U}_{m}\|\mathrm{softmax}(\mathbf{z})) is biased by trajectory length, since longer trajectories accumulate more negative log-probability. Conversely, length normalization z i / | τ i | z_{i}/|	au_{i}| admits a padding shortcut, as high-probability filler tokens can increase the normalized score without actually strengthening the underlying strategy. We therefore measure each strategy’s relative growth against a fixed 