d ( Rein et al., 2024 ) , and LiveCodeBench (v6) ( Jain et al., 2024 ) , covering mathematical, scientific, and code reasoning. We compare Mesh Learning with GRPO, DAPO ( Yu et al., 2025 ) , GSPO ( Zheng et al., 2025 ) , and GRPO with KL or JS regularization ( Li et al., 2026 ) . 5.2 Main Results Table 2: Main results and controlled ablations (Mean@4 Accuracy %). CP variants isolate the effect of Coach Prompting, while m = 3 m=3 and m = 4 m=4 compare different strategy capacities. Method Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct Qwen-3-4B AIME26 AIME25 MATH-500 GPQA AIME26 AIME25 MATH-500 GPQA Standard RLVR Training Baseline 3.3 5.0 64.8 13.7 13.3 21.6 62.5 13.7 GRPO 9.2 13.3 69.6 33.4 43.3 40.0 81.6 38.9 DAPO 8.3 6.7 70.5 34.1 43.3 40.8 84.3 44.5 GSPO 9.2 5.8 71.4 34.3 41.6 38.3 79.8 45.8 GRPO + KL 9.2 13.3 71.8 31.6 43.3 40.0 82.2 42.3 GRPO + JS 10.0 6.4 71.4 28.5 40.0 43.3 81.8 44.8 Controlled with Coach Prompting (CP) GRPO + CP 10.0 13.3 69.6 31.7 43.3 40.0 81.6 37.8 DAPO + CP 7.5 5.8 69.7 26.3 36.6 33.3 84.5 42.3 GSPO + CP 6.7 8.3 68.5 32.1 41.7 33.3 85.3 43.7 GRPO + KL + CP 4.4 8.3 71.8 31.9 40.4 38.3 78.5 33.2 GRPO + JS + CP 10.0 7.8 72.0 30.7 40.0 41.4 80.4 30.7 Mesh Learning ( m = 3 m=3 ) 10.3 16.3 72.4 36.7 52.5 43.7 84.8 44.6 Mesh Learning ( m = 4 m=4 ) 13.3 16.7 73.4 36.7 56.7 48.3 86.8 47.9 Overall Performance. Table 2 summarizes results across two models and four benchmarks. We highlight two observations: 1) Mesh Learning consistently improves reasoning performance. With the default m = 4 m=4 , Mesh Learning achieves the best performance across all eight model–benchmark settings. Compared with the strongest RLVR baseline in each setting, Mesh Learning improves Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct by 1.6 1.6 – 3.4 3.4 pp and Qwen3-4B by 2.1 2.1 – 13.4 13.4 pp. The largest gain occurs on AIME26 with Qwen3-4B, improving accuracy from 43.3 % 43.3\% to 56.7 % 56.7\% . 2) We observe consistent gains across tasks and model scales. Mesh Learning improves challenging competition-level benchmarks (AIME25/26) and broader mathematical and scientific reasoning benchmarks (MATH-500 and GPQA-Diamond). These gains hold across Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct and Qwen3-4B, indicating that the benefit is not specific to a single model or task. Trajectory-level examples are provided in Appendix E , while ablations on Coach Prompting and strategy number m m are presented in Sec. 5.3 . Figure 7: Training dynamics of accuracy (top) and MEI (bottom). Baselines can exhibit late-stage collapse with rising MEI, while Mesh Learning maintains stable accuracy and low MEI. Figure 8: Pass@ k k performance on AIME25 and AIME26. Across increasing inference budgets, Mesh Learning consistently outperforms all baselines on both models. Training Dynamics. Fig. 7 shows that several RLVR baselines improve but later suffer abrupt accuracy collapse, accompanied by a sharp rise in MEI beyond the 3 ​ σ 3\sigma threshold, while Mesh Learning keeps MEI low and consistently attains higher accuracy. GPQA is an exception: four choices imply γ ≈ 1 \gamma\approx 1 , violating Theorem 2 ’s condition, so strategy concentration need not induce collapse. Capability Frontier. To examine whether these gains persist under larger inference budgets, we report Pass@ k k on AIME25 and AIME26 for k ∈ { 2 , 8 , 32 , 128 } k\in\{2,8,32,128\} . As shown in Fig. 8 , Mesh Learning consistently outperforms all baselines across both models, with its advantage remaining substantial as k k increases. In particular, the gains at Pass@128 reach 6.9 6.9 and 7.3 7.3 pp for Qwen2.5-7B and 32.6 32.6 and 28.8 28.8 pp for Qwen3-4B on AIME26 and AIME25, respectively. These results indicate that Mesh Learning improves not only single-budget accuracy but also the attainable performance under substantially larger sampling budgets. Table 3: Cross-family generalization on Phi-4-mini-reasoning (Mean@4 Accuracy %). Method AIME26 AIME25 MATH-500 GPQA GRPO 22.3 24.1 79.9 18.4 DAPO 25.0 23.4 77.3 23.0 Mesh Learning 33.3 30.8 83.2 34.5 Cross Family Generalization. Beyond Qwen3-4B and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, we further evaluate Mesh Learning on Phi-4-mini-reasoning to examine transfer across model families. As shown in Table 3 , Mesh Learning consistently outperforms both GRPO and DAPO across all four benchmarks, achieving gains over the stronger baseline of 8.3 8.3 , 6.7 6.7 , 3.3 3.3 , and 11.5 11.5 pp on AIME26, AIME25, MATH-500, and GPQA-Diamond, respectively. These results provide additional evidence that the effectiveness of Mesh Learning is not specific to the Qwen family. Table 4: LiveCodeBench v6 (Mean@4 Accuracy %) Model Baseline GRPO DAPO Mesh Learning Qwen-3-4B 30.5 48.4 52.2 56.4 Phi-4-mini-reasoning 24.8 31.5 32.7 36.3 Generalization to Coding Tasks. To evaluate generalization beyond math reasoning, we extend experiments to LiveCodeBench v6. Table 4 shows that Mesh Learning reaches 56.4% on Qwen3-4B and 36.3% on Phi-4-mini-reasoning, outperforming DAPO by 4.2 pp and 3.6 pp, suggesting that its benefits transfer to coding tasks. 5.3 Ablation Studies Ablation for Coach-Prompting Priors. Since Coach Prompting provides extra strategy information, we compare each RLVR baseline with a CP-only counterpart using the same prefixes but without Strategy-Balancing Regularization. As shown in Table 2 , CP alone brings little or no improvement, while full Mesh Learning consistently performs better. This indicates that the gains do not come from stronger strategy priors alone, but from preserving these strategies during optimization. Number of Strategies. As shown in Table 2 , increasing m m from 3 3 to 4 4 leads to only small differences overall, showing that Mesh Learning is not sensitive to modest changes in m m . However, we found that an overly small m m limits the number of strategies exposed during training and therefore restricts the strategy capacity that can be preserved. We use m = 4 m=4 by default. 6 Related Work RLVR optimization. Following GRPO ( Shao et al., 2024 ) , substantial work has improved RLVR through better sampling, clipping, importance weighting, and optimization objectives, including DAPO ( Yu et al., 2025 ) , CISPO ( Chen et al., 2025 ) , GSPO ( Zheng et al., 2025 ) , SAPO ( Gao et al., 2025 ) , Dr. GRPO ( Liu et al., 2025 ) , VAPO ( Yue et al., 2025 ) , REINFORCE++ ( Hu et al., 2025 ) , GMPO ( Zhao et al., 2026 ) and FAPO ( Ding et al., 2026 ) . These methods primarily improve RLVR’s optimization behavior, whereas we focus on the mechanism underlying its late-stage collapse. Understanding and Preventing Collapse. Recent work has documented collapse during prolonged RLVR training. Liang et al. (2026) show that Pass@1 improvements can coincide with reduced generation diversity and degraded high- k k Pass@ k k , while Nguyen et al. (2025) attribute reasoning-boundary shrinkage to negative interference and winner-take-all dynamics. Yuan et al. (2026) link diversity collapse to continued optimization on already-saturated problems, and M-GRPO ( Bai et al., 2025 ) studies abrupt performance collapse under long-horizon training. A common remedy is to constrain policy drift with fixed-reference KL regularization ( Shao et al., 2024 ) , while alternative divergences such as forward KL and JS have been shown to better preserve solution diversity ( Li et al., 2026 ) . In contrast, we provide a strategy-level theoretical account: starting from trajectory-level update interactions, we derive RLVR’s tendency toward strategy concentration and a complementary lower bound on the strategy capacity required for nontrivial task performance. Their tension directly explains catastrophic collapse and motivates Mesh Learning. 7 Conclusion This work characterizes, anticipates, and mitigates catastrophic collapse in RLVR. We develop a strategy-level theory showing that RLVR progressively concentrates strategy mass, while successful reasoning requires sufficient strategy capacity; their conflict results collapse. This analysis further yields MEI as a lightweight online warning signal. We propose Mesh Learning , combining Coach Prompting and Strategy-Balancing Regularization to expose and preserve diverse strategies during optimization. Experiments across AIME26, AIME25, MATH-500, GPQA, and LiveCodeBench consistently demonstrate its effectiveness across Qwen and Phi model families. In conclusion, All Work And No Play Makes Jack A Dull Boy . RLVR may trade away strategy diversity for continued gains in reward, eventually exhausting the capacity required for further meaningful optimization. Our results highlight strategy preservation as a key principle for stable and effective RLVR. AI use statement In this work, we used generative AI tools to assist with language polishing, code implementation, and the refinement and correctness checking of theorem proofs. Generative AI tools were not used to independently write the manuscript from scratch or to generate citations or bibliographic references. All AI-assisted code was reviewed, verified, and tested by the authors. All theorem statements and proofs, including those for which generative AI tools were used as an auxiliary correctness check, were independently examined and verified by the authors. No citations were generated by generative AI tools, and all references included in the paper were manually checked by the authors to confirm their authenticity and relevance. We have reviewed all AI-assisted work and take full responsibility for the final content of this work, including all text, claims, proofs, code, and other artifacts produced with the aid of generative AI. Reproducibility statement All code and scripts are available in our anonymous GitHub repository at GitHub . All datasets required for training, including those processed with our Coach Prompting procedure, are provided in the supplementary materials as data.zip, except for the coding dataset, which is too large to include in the submission. References Abdin et al. (2025) M. Abdin, S. Agarwal, A. Awadallah, V. Balachandran, H. Beh